## Context

The session records sit under every reporter and every reader. The session's own server appends the agent's reports; Claude Code's hook processes fire on every prompt and tool call, sometimes several at once; another session's end request may mark this one ended. The observer, the start tool's halt check, the home list and the delete control all read the same records back and ask the same question of them: is this session still open?

Two identities meet here. Claude Code names a session by its own id; the agent's reports name the observer session. Until something joins them, a hook's presence belongs to a session no report names. Logs also outlive the format: sessions recorded before pipelines were named must still read in full.

## Strategy

**One writer per file, three kinds of file.** The report log is appended only by its session's server; presence is replaced whole by each hook call, the newest hook time winning a race; an ended mark is written once. Keeping presence and marks out of the log means a hook firing mid-append can never split a line, and reading presence never parses a log.

**The recorder stamps; one strict schema checks everywhere.** Time, session and order are stamped by the recorder, never supplied by the agent, and the same schema validates at append, at read and at the observer's intake. A line that fails is skipped alone. A line from before pipelines were named is valid and belongs to no pipeline: a missing name is history, not corruption.

**Refuse after the end, before anything is written.** Recording checks the session's ended mark first; once ended, nothing is appended and nothing is posted. Otherwise it appends, then posts with every failure swallowed, and the reply names the session and says whether the observer received it, which is all the agent needs to decide on a restart.

**Join the two identities through the session-start reply.** The reply to the session-start report names the observer session in a form the hook can read, and the hook that fires after that call stores it in the host session's presence. Unjoined presence stays under the host's id and still reads as an open session, so a session that never reported is never invisible.

**One liveness rule, asked by every reader.** The ended mark wins, then presence's last state, then, with no presence at all, the reported events. Last activity also counts the transcript's last write, because one long step writes the transcript while no hook fires. The halt check, home list and delete refusal all call this one rule, so the page and the controls never disagree.

**Delete is narrow; ending deletes nothing.** Deleting removes one ended session's log, presence and mark and nothing else; a log that cannot be read cannot be judged, so it is refused. Ending writes a mark and keeps every record, so a resumed pipeline can still count what that session finished.

## Good examples

**Two hooks at once.** Two tool calls finish together and both hook processes replace the session's presence; the later hook time wins, and the report log is untouched.

**An old log.** A session recorded before pipelines were named reads with every event, belonging to no pipeline; one truncated line in it is skipped and the events after it still show.

**A report after the end.** The user ended a stuck session from a new one. The stuck session wakes and reports a build step; the report is refused, nothing is appended, and its rail does not move.

## Bad examples

**Presence appended to the log.** The hook writes presence as lines into the report log; a hook firing during an append leaves a half line, and the session silently loses an event.

**Two liveness rules.** The home list judges from presence while the delete control still uses report age, so a session shown ended cannot be deleted, or one shown open is.

**Old logs rejected.** A line without a pipeline name is treated as malformed, and a month of history disappears from every project.

